// ATTACK (red team round 5, concurrency lens): does the codegen-inserted
// mutating-closure serialization lock (LEDGER item 7b / 11(a),
// kryos_closure_lock_acquire/kryos_mutex_lock in
// kryos-stdlib-native/src/sync_prims.rs) interact safely with the
// COOPERATIVE async executor (kryos-rt/src/executor.rs), or does holding
// that lock across a coop-yield point permanently wedge BOTH the real
// atomic mutex AND the executor's single-active-task baton?
//
// The closure thunk is: kryos_closure_lock_acquire(lock) -> call underlying
// fn body -> store-back -> kryos_closure_lock_release(lock). If the
// underlying body calls a blocking op that yields the coop baton
// (`sleep_ms`, `await`, `http_get`, ... -- docs/09-concurrency.md: "A
// blocking I/O op ... called inside an async task yields the scheduler for
// the duration of the syscall") WHILE the lock is held, task A's OS thread
// physically sleeps but the lock (both the thread-local reentrancy marker
// AND the real AtomicBool in sync_prims.rs) stays held the whole time.
//
// The executor hands the baton to the next ready task (task B). If task B
// then calls the SAME shared closure, `kryos_closure_lock_acquire` sees a
// DIFFERENT thread (not self-reentrancy, so no panic) and falls through to
// the real `kryos_mutex_lock` CAS spin -- which is a bare
// spin-then-yield_now() loop that only yields the OS SCHEDULER, never the
// COOP BATON (it has no idea a cooperative executor exists). Task B is
// therefore stuck spinning forever on its own OS thread while, from the
// coop scheduler's point of view, task B still "holds the baton" (it never
// calls kryos_coop_yield or returns). Task A, meanwhile, is parked waiting
// for the baton to come back after its sleep -- which the scheduler can
// never grant, because task B never releases it. Neither task can ever
// finish: task A cannot resume to unlock the real mutex, task B cannot ever
// win the CAS because task A never gets to run again to release it. This is
// a genuine permanent hang/livelock (task B spins at ~100% of one core
// forever; the process never exits) with ZERO throw/panic/reentrancy
// involved -- just two ordinary async tasks sharing one stateful closure,
// the documented, encouraged idiom ("Sharing is now safe" in
// docs/09-concurrency.md, which only demonstrates OS-thread `spawn`
// contention, never coop-task contention).
//
// Control: `attack_closure_lock_coop_yield_deadlock_control.kry` runs the
// identical program but with the `sleep_ms` call REMOVED from the closure
// body (no yield point while the lock is held) -- if the control finishes
// fast and this one hangs, the yield-while-locked interaction is the cause,
// not coop_spawn/shared-closure contention in general.
//
// CONFIRMED (both backends, isolated from machine contention by re-running
// after killing stray kryos.exe and comparing against the control at the
// same time): this file HANGS PERMANENTLY. `kryos run` (Cranelift JIT):
// 5/5 runs each printed "about to coop_run" then never returned, still
// alive and unresponsive past 25s per run (`timeout 25` rc=124 every time);
// the sibling control ran 5/5 to completion in ~4s each with deterministic
// output `order: A=1 B=3`. `kryos build --release` (LLVM AOT): the built
// binary reproduced the same hang (rc=124 at 25s) while the AOT-built
// control completed normally (`order: A=1 B=3`, rc=0). A `Get-Process`
// check on one hung JIT run showed ~0.125s of CPU time consumed across
// 70+ seconds of wall time -- the process is genuinely PARKED (a lost
// baton / condvar deadlock), not a hot CAS spin, so this is a true
// permanent hang, not a livelock burning a core.
fn caller(f: fn(i64) -> i64, n: i64, tag: str) -> i64 {
    let r = f(n)
    coop_record(tag + "=" + to_string(r))
    return r
}

fn main() {
    let mut counter: i64 = 0
    let bump = |n: i64| {
        counter = counter + n
        sleep_ms(80)
        counter
    }
    coop_reset()
    coop_spawn(caller(bump, 1, "A"))
    coop_spawn(caller(bump, 2, "B"))
    println("about to coop_run -- if the closure lock deadlocks the coop baton, this HANGS FOREVER")
    coop_run()
    println("coop_run returned, counter=" + to_string(counter))
    println("order: " + coop_order())
}
